      {
        "id": 87020,
        "name": "Envisioner",
        "username": "Envisioner",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/9de053/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2014-01-20T04:15:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Silverleaf\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/silverleaf/48/2332_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Silverleaf:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I've almost certainly clocked more than 300 games in the 13 months I've had Sentinels (playing online means I get in more games than the \"average\" player), and like Ronway I'm nowhere near burnout. There has to be literally billions of different hero/villain/environment combinations, and even with the exact same setup you'll see different cards and a different game. Not to mention promos…I believe randomising everything helps a lot. If you always play Haka or prefer to take Tempest every time you face Voss, you're more likely to get bored.&nbsp;</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I've played almost 100 games in 5-6 months.&nbsp; And I play different heroes nearly every time (for the Nemesis beatdown I played 8 games using the same heroes for 3 and then 5 of them, but that was an exception to my usual rule).&nbsp; Like I said, I'm not burned out yet, but I suspect I will be eventually, if only during the long cycles between expansions coming out (Vengeance is arriving before I'm entirely tired of everything before it, and those five new heroes will last me a good long while, but the next expansion is probably going to add only two new heroes, and so I'll likely be starting to lose interest by the time its successor is visible on the horizon).&nbsp; And the billions of combinations are only a technicality; realistically, the difference between Haka/Wraith/Tempest vs. Voss and Haka/Wraith/Tempest vs. Rat is not that huge, so in more meaningful terms (IMO), the number of possible games is more like the 200+ villain/environment combinations, times about 10 to 20 for the various ever-shifting roster of hero teams.&nbsp; It's likely that playing a lot of solitaire contributed to the sense of burnout, which is why I've cut way back on it…if I'm only playing one hero instead of an entire team, it helps provide more variety.&nbsp; But I'm still beginning to feel as though most of the villains are a solved puzzle.&nbsp; If you're against Apostate, kill his Relics.&nbsp; If you're against Iron Legacy, bring Ongoing destruction.&nbsp; If you're against Plague Rat, prevent damage.&nbsp; I have to work a bit to stave off the first stages of boredom, and that process will gradually increase…I'll need to come up with more elaborate scenario rules and so forth, in order to keep my interest up.</p>",
        "post_number": 26,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:11:10.925Z",
